James inquired about the standard rate for evaluating benefit-cost ratios for climate adaptation projects. The response clarified that these evaluations involve assessing the net present value of benefits and costs, typically using a discount rate ranging from 1% to 7%. It emphasized the importance of a lower rate (around 2%-3%) to account for long-term impacts and intergenerational equity, giving greater weight to future benefits. The discussion highlighted the need for selecting an appropriate rate that aligns with project objectives and broader climate policies, while also considering methodologies suggested by specific organizations or research bodies for tailored evaluations.
